title The backend beside the backend -- the recommendation, and the measurement that decided it
prompt Keaton asked what I recommend, on the two doors the probe opened: AppKit-in-Rye against SwiftUI-in-Swift, and whether skate/ yonders toward iOS next.
think One measurement decided the first door. brushstroke/wayland_seed.rye is 1,315 lines whose own header reads "attach one SHM buffer filled from a cell grid, commit once, wait for the frame callback, exit cleanly" -- and the macOS probe written tonight is that same program in about two hundred lines. Skate is not a second application; it is Brushstroke's macOS backend, and the tree already built the harder one.
think So the recommendation is AppKit through Rye, framed as a backend beside the Wayland backend rather than as a rewrite. SwiftUI buys a hosting view for a surface that draws its own pixels, which is near zero, against a measured cost the tree has already paid once in REDS %295.
think The second door I recommend NOT taking next, and the reason is the finishing edge rather than doubt: Mind is mid-flight in skate/ with three proven candidates today, and moving its lane costs a supervisor update, a re-pin, and a stalled star to buy tidiness. The crux worth shipping first is the second probe -- a real Brushstroke cell grid rendered through the AppKit path with one event coming back -- after which the yonder is bookkeeping rather than a bet.
obs Honest about scale: a window is a lap, a backend is a quest. Event handling wants a view class registered at runtime through objc_allocateClassPair, and resize, retina scaling, redraw, and a bundle all follow. The evidence it is tractable is that the Wayland twin already stands, and it was the harder protocol.
obs What would invert the whole recommendation, named plainly: wanting Liquid Glass specifically. That is SwiftUI's own ground, and no amount of runtime reach substitutes for it.
file active-designing/20260828-215659_the-window-rye-raised.md the finding this recommendation rests on
recommend check-in the second probe (a real cell grid through AppKit, one event back) is the crux I would take next; the yonder follows it cheaply, and both stay Keaton's word
